<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh Capet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 940–996</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh Capet was Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and, from 987, King of the Franks, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, he held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and was elected king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> after the death of the last Carolingian king, Louis V. His royal power was limited, but by crowning his son Robert as co-king he secured a dynasty whose branches ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-capet.jpg" alt="Hugh Capet" width="227" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeThéodorus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SceauHuguesCapet_(cropped).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
